7 capex by 18% and Norvane by 12%.\n  - Data-centre connection requests are expected to grow 9% in 2027, which is slower than in 2026.\n  - These are capex plans and connection requests, not equipment orders.\n- **Financing:** The funding of that capex is shaky, and it hits VLTX's biggest customer.\n  - Pellham withdrew a C$2.4B equity raise and will cut 2027 transformer orders by about 40%. It will also defer two substation programmes.\n  - Norvane priced a C$1.1B bond comfortably and looks able to fund its plan through debt.\n  - Utility bond spreads widened 30bp to 165bp in Q3 2026, and two utility share offerings were cut back.\n- **Concentration:** Utilities are 62% of VLTX's orders. Pellham is 35% of those utility orders and Norvane is 21%. The Pellham order cut therefore matters a lot for VLTX.\n- **Pricing:** The LEAPS are fairly priced, not cheap. Implied volatility is 27% against a 2-year median of 26%, and skew is flat, so there is no pricing edge. This check didn't cover a specific strike, expiry or premium.\n\nThe demand picture is positive overall, but the Pellham cut is a near-term negative for VLTX orders that the option pricing doesn't compensate for.\n\nI'd reconsider if either of these happens:\n- Equity markets recover, or Pellham restores its orders.\n- LEAPS implied volatility drops meaningfully below its median.\n\nFull detail is in n2.r1, n3.r1 and n4.r1.","ttft_ms":2260,"type":"result","duration_ms":50088,"uuid":"8af23978-9b98-4346-989e-4bb045140756","ttft_stream_ms":1232,"time_to_request_ms":135,"time_to_request_phases_ms":{"other":15,"system_prompt":8,"resume_other":0,"process_user_input":9,"input_attachments":23,"input_hooks":5,"conversation_append":0,"transcript_persist":12,"tool_pool_refresh":0,"init_message":10,"init_frame_send":4,"engine_pickup":1,"loop_entry":7,"message_prep":0,"model_config_wait":1,"autocompact":5,"request_announcements":15,"query_setup":1,"model_gates":1,"tool_schema_build":8,"message_normalization":3,"client_creat